WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.000
Let's talk about space

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.640
and history being made on the launch pad.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.440
“3 ... 2 ... 1 ....”

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.080
"America has launched."

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.480
For the first time ever

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.400
a private company, SpaceX

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.880
has put people into orbit.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.560
Launching a new capsule, a new rocket

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.400
it's a major, major thing.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.480
It’s all about building a commercial space industry.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.560
Going back to the Moon.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.760
We should become a multi-planet civilisation.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.360
Even if it’s just a science base.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.760
Going to Mars.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.040
And back here on Earth

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.920
hypersonic flights at speeds

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.240
that’ll make the Concorde look like a Cessna.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.440
 look like a Cessna.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.920
But what makes this launch special?

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.560
Why is all this money being spent on space?

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.560
And do we really need to fly New York to Shanghai

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.320
in 39 minutes?

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.280
Billionaires dream big.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.440
And that’s Elon Musk to a T.
And he wants us to dream big too.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.400
There are many troubles in the world.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.360
These are important and we need to solve them.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.160
But we also need things that make us excited to be alive.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.160
Everything is a stepping stone to the next step

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.240
to the next technological feat.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.400
So let's understand: Elon Musk's goal

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.440
is to build, by 2050, a city on Mars that will have 

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.760
a population of a million people.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.560
Pretty amazing considering that not long ago

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.520
about the only things flying were hot air balloons

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.040
and gliders made of wood.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.560
But that changed in 1903 with the Wright brothers. 

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.080
Orville and Wilbur flew the first motorised plane.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.080
And after that — well, things took off.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.440
The F 13 was the first all-metal commercial plane.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.240
It carried four passengers.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.880
The ’40s saw the rise of the Constellation 

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.880
and the fall of speed records.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.280
Then came the Comet. 

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.800
The world's first commercial jet airliner.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.680
It crashed a few times because of stress fractures

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.760
around its square windows.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.960
It’s why windows on planes today are oval.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.160
The ’70s brought the supersonic Concorde.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.480
In the early 2000s the Airbus A380 and 787 Dreamliner

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.200
made it cheaper for airlines to fly people around.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.600
And today there’s Elon Musk and his plans for the Starship

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.200
to fly us to other planets — or even between cities

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.000
at insane speeds.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:21.720
The critical breakthrough that’s needed

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.960
The critical breakthrough that’s needed

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.280
for us to become a 
spacefaring civilisation

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.040
is to make space travel like air travel.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.560
We’ll talk about 
intercity commutes 

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.280
on rockets a little later.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.800
But let’s talk about this SpaceX launch first. 

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.480
“Lift-off!”

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.000
Up until now only governments

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.800
in the US, Russia and China have been able

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.560
to fly people into space.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.120
So this is the first commercial company

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.200
to take people into low-Earth orbit.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.880
And this is a test mission called Demo-2.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.640
It’s also — as NASA likes to remind us

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.920
the first launch of American astronauts

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.440
the first launch of American astronauts

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.720
from American soil

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.280
in almost a decade.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.800
Astronauts Bob Behnken 
and Doug Hurley

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.000
rode up in a capsule 
called the Crew Dragon.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.920
It’s like a big SUV that seats up to seven.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.960
But it’s less tin can Apollo 11, more sleek modern kitchen

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.360
with touchscreens.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.800
Bad weather delayed the launch by a few days.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.320
“Unfortunately we are not going to launch today.”

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.960
But then there was lift-off ...

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.720
Lift-off! Go NASA, go SpaceX, godspeed Bob and Doug!

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.560
from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.280
from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.600
to the International Space Station, or the ISS

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.400
where Behnken and Hurley will stay up to four months.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.240
We’re given the honour 
to name this capsule.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.840
Without further ado
we would like to 

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.400
welcome you aboard
capsule Endeavour. 

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.800
It’s historic. And sure, it’s something to celebrate.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.440
But what’s in it for SpaceX? And NASA?

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.840
Well privatised space flight means the US

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.880
doesn’t have to spend as many tax dollars

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.960
to transport cargo and people to the ISS.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.400
Since NASA retired the Space Shuttle in 2011

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.720
We sure hope that everybody was able to take

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.640
just a little part of the journey with us.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.360
it’s had to rely on Russian Soyuz rockets

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.800
to get to the space station.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
It costs roughly $85 million a seat

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.400
to send a NASA astronaut up on the Soyuz.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.400
SpaceX is going to charge about $55 million per seat.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.000
NASA also saves money by getting private companies

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.400
like SpaceX and Boeing to compete for contracts.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.800
They take on the big expenses of innovation and testing

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.240
and it drives down the costs for NASA.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.760
This rocket, you know — nuts to bolts, top to tail 

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.520
all of it is owned by a commercial entity.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.480
Not one part of it is owned by NASA or the US government

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.200
except for, well, 
the astronauts  

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.720
and the patches on their chest.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.920
Musk started his company with $100 million 

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.080
of his own cash. That was 18 years ago.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.600
Today his private company is reportedly worth 

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.080
as much as $36 billion. 

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.320
So what does that tell you? 

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.800
There’s money to be made in space.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.440
In 2018 hundreds of billions of dollars were spent

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.800
on things like satellite-based entertainment

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.160
mapping and weather.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.960
In 20 years people think space-related goods

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.640
and services could be worth more than a trillion.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.120
That trillion-dollar figure includes projects like

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.280
a new satellite-based internet, space tourism and mining

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.000
on mineral-rich asteroids and on the Moon.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.600
What becomes more valuable than gold is water. 

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.600
And water has been discovered on the Moon.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.640
And that's what everyone is trying to race to, to get at.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.840
And it’s not just about water to drink.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.840
People are looking at the Moon as a potential

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.160
gas station on the way to Mars.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:46.240
Because when you split water into oxygen and hydrogen

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.440
and liquify it you get rocket fuel.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.320
And the US isn’t the only country 

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.800
with plans for the Moon.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.360
Russia’s going. China and India are too. 

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.600
The thing is, is that whoever really gets there first

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.880
really gets to decide the rules.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.760
So there are plenty of business incentives.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.120
And companies like SpaceX and Boeing

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.480
well, they want to be the airlines to space.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.480
But first they have to show they can do it safely.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.360
And to do that they’ve had to learn 

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.880
from some spectacular failures.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.920
"And we appear to have had a launch vehicle failure."

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.640
If you’re going to have a problem with engines

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.520
it's better to have it on the ground.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.520
“Oooh! Oh no!”

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.160
As anyone in space will tell you 

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.160
all failures — while really, really awful

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.480
as long as they don't kill anybody 

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.320
they're incredible learning experiences.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.680
Boeing, which is a SpaceX competitor

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.320
 is still learning too.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.120
It’s developing its own spacecraft

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.280
for astronauts called the Starliner.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.120
But it’s had its problems.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:04.160
"And lift-off. The rise of Starliner — a new era of human space flight."

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.120
Last December a test flight without a crew got lost

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.920
on the way to the space station because of 

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.440
a software glitch.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.040
Boeing is going to be back — pretty sure of that.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.200
But how soon is anybody's guess.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.160
For SpaceX it’s not just about getting to space.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.640
Because on top of everything else Elon Musk also wants

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.320
to rocket civilians between cities in no time at all.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.160
A commercial flight from New York to Shanghai 

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.040
in 39 minutes.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.240
“Anywhere on Earth in under an hour”

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.080
that’s their sales pitch.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.560
So far there’s no timetable for this adventure. 

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.000
It may never even happen.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.560
But people are already asking questions

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.240
like: Imagine your carbon footprint on one of those flights?

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.800
Not to mention the jetlag.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.520
I think it will be just a white elephant in the end. 

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.760
Why do we need to fly like that?

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:56.480
We have to apply our technology 

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.320
for the benefit of humanity.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.560
Who knows? Humanity might benefit in the end.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.840
But right now there are some powerful commercial drivers

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.440
that have some people saying: 

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.840
Let’s keep an eye on this space race.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:13.560
I can see a future of an enormous amount of countries

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.480
private enterprises that go into space.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:18.960
Space tourism. Hotels on the Moon. 

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.280
Exploitation of the 
Moon for minerals.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.000
“We have a lift-off!”

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.840
You have to remember that the first plaque that was

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.720
left on the Moon — it's still there — by the Apollo 11 said:

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.240
“We Came in Peace for all Mankind.”

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:34.560
And that has to continue.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:38.880
There’s a famous picture of Earth called the Pale Blue Dot.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.200
It was taken 30 years ago by a space probe called Voyager 1

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.520
6 billion kilometres away.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.280
In it the Earth is about the size of a pixel

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.120
you can barely see us.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.200
But it was enough to inspire Carl Sagan, the cosmologist.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.640
For him that picture 

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.000
“underscores our responsibility to deal 

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.120
more kindly with one another, and to preserve and cherish 

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.240
the pale blue dot — the only home we've ever known.”

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.040
If anything those words mean more today 
than they ever did.

